module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.control_plane_acknowledgement_minted_as_effect

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data control_plane_acknowledgement_minted_as_effect: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "control_plane_acknowledgement_minted_as_effect" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**an accepted command, a desired-state readback, or an actuator acknowledgement cannot construct a terminal effect ON THE ACTED-UPON SUBJECT; the control plane can only witness that it ISSUED something** (INVALID STATE: a terminal naming an effect at the subject -- the host booted, the device reimaged, the tenant provisioned -- is minted from facts that all live on the CONTROL side of the boundary: the request was accepted, a setting reads back as requested, an actuator returned success. Each of those is true of a request that the subject then ignored, could not satisfy, or never received, so the terminal is indistinguishable between a working actuation and one that provably did nothing. DISTINGUISHING FACTS, and they are what separate this from the ordinary absence of a check: every precondition genuinely HELD, so no arm is lying and no diagnostic fires; the readback really does render the requested value; the power/apply/deploy call really was accepted. The defect is not a missing validation but a CATEGORY SUBSTITUTION -- a precondition rendered as an effect -- which is why it survives review: a reviewer checking `is the readback verified?` finds yes. It is DESIGN section 5's specification-without-execution at the boundary between a control plane and the thing it controls. EVIDENCE, EXECUTED, gunbc#10965 on Mt. Collins unit 1 (2026-09-10/11): `gunbc.machine_intake_oob_boot_handoff` mints `BootHandoffCompleted { selected: device }` immediately after the power action, from (i) boot parameter 5 reading back the expected rendering and (ii) the chassis power action being accepted. Over five attempts against a real host, BOTH held EVERY TIME -- the selector genuinely read `Force Boot from CD/DVD`, the reset genuinely was accepted -- while the host booted the intended medium on only some of them, falling through to PXE and a UEFI shell on the others. The model reported a completed handoff on attempts that demonstrably booted nothing. WHAT IT COST, WHICH IS THE ARGUMENT FOR THE ROW: IPMI boot parameter 5 encodes TWO decisions, the device selector AND the BIOS boot type (byte 1 bit 5, 0 = PC-compatible/legacy, 1 = EFI). The modelled path carried only the selector, so every handoff silently requested the DEFAULT boot type, which reads back `BIOS PC Compatible (legacy) boot`; aarch64 has no legacy boot path, so the request was unsatisfiable. A two-field decision hid behind a one-field check for an entire intake lane, and the reason nothing surfaced it is precisely this class: the terminal said Completed on every failed attempt, so there was no signal to investigate. The readback check even PASSED on the half it covered -- `mtcollins1_boot_readback_rendering` asserts the expected selector string and matched it -- which is what makes proximity-to-a-check so misleading here. RECOGNITION RULE: for any terminal whose NAME asserts something about the subject, ask which side of the boundary each of its constructing facts was observed on. If every one of them is a control-plane fact -- request accepted, setting reads back, actuator returned 0 -- the terminal is a claim the evidence cannot support, whatever its name says. The mirror question: could this terminal be constructed for a subject that was powered off, unplugged, or absent? If yes, it is naming the wrong thing. THE REPAIR IS A SPLIT, NOT A STRONGER CHECK: the control-plane facts construct a control-plane terminal -- `BootControlIssued` / `RestartIssued` / `ApplyRequested` -- and the subject-level terminal requires INDEPENDENT DOWNSTREAM OBSERVATION originating at the subject. For boot delivery the delivered image's own receipt landing is such an observation and is far stronger than console scraping, which on this controller proved unreliable (SOL closed mid-POST and later accepted keystrokes while returning nothing). RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the module's own comment already said it does not observe what booted, and the terminal was named Completed anyway, so the knowledge was present and unenforced. CEILING: structurally impossible, because a terminal that can only be constructed from a subject-originating observation has no constructor reachable from control-plane facts alone. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability: a boot-delivery model in which the subject-level terminal takes a host-originating attestation as a CONSTRUCTOR ARGUMENT -- SUFFICIENT FOR every actuation in machine-intake that currently concludes from an accepted command, not merely for the IPMI boot path. Fixing the boot-type argv WITHOUT this split would close the instance and leave the class: the next unsatisfiable request would again be reported as completed.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
